   102	## 2. Local braid monodromy at `A_{2k-1}` and at a vertical tangency
   103	
   104	Fix a generic `x_0`, `F = {x_0} x C`, `F cap D = d` points, `pi_1(F - D) = F_d` free on
   105	meridians `xi_1, ..., xi_d`. The braid monodromy `rho: pi_1(C - Delta, x_0) -> B_d`
   106	(`Delta` = critical values of `pr|_D`) acts on `F_d` by the Artin action
   107	`sigma_i: xi_i -> xi_i xi_{i+1} xi_i^{-1}, xi_{i+1} -> xi_i`, and Zariski–van Kampen
   108	gives
   109	
   110	```text
   111	pi_1(C^2 - D) = < xi_1,...,xi_d | xi_j = rho(g_i)(xi_j),  all i, j > .
   112	```
   113	
   114	No relation at infinity is imposed: `pr|_D` is proper, so `C^2 - D` is the total space of
   115	the ZvK presentation as displayed. (The extra relation `xi_d...xi_1 = 1` presents
   116	`pi_1(P^2 - Dbar)`, which is *not* the group in question — cf. Oka's Remark 3, quoted in
   117	the acquisition §2 with the cuspidal-cubic witness.)
   118	
   119	**Local braids, re-derived here (the acquisition's §4 identification is an inference and
   120	integration §3 forbids consuming it as a source).**
   121	
   122	*Vertical tangency.* A simple vertical tangency at a smooth point of `D` has local model
   123	`x - x_0 = y^2`, two roots `y = +- (x-x_0)^{1/2}`, which exchange once as `x` circles
   124	`x_0`: local braid `sigma_i`. Relations `xi_i = sigma_i(xi_i)`,
   125	`xi_{i+1} = sigma_i(xi_{i+1})` both reduce to
   126	
   127	```text
   128	(T)      xi_i = xi_{i+1} .
   129	```
   130	
   131	*`A_{2k-1}`.* Two smooth branches of contact `k`: after an analytic change of the
   132	`y`-coordinate the germ is `y = +- x^k` (common tangent `y = 0`, and the vertical line
   133	`x = 0` is transverse to both branches, meeting `D` with multiplicity `2` — so the fibre
   134	count drops by exactly one, as for a node). As `x` circles the origin once,
   135	`x^k -> x^k` and `-x^k -> -x^k`: the two roots do **not** exchange but wind `k` times
   136	around each other. Local braid `sigma_i^{2k}`; equivalently the local link is the
   137	`(2, 2k)` torus link. Writing `w = xi_i xi_{i+1}`, `sigma_i^{2k}` conjugates both `xi_i`
   138	and `xi_{i+1}` by `w^k`, so the relations are the single condition
   139	
   140	```text
   141	(N_k)    [ (xi_i xi_{i+1})^k , xi_i ] = 1 ,
   142	```
   143	
   144	`k = 1` being ordinary commutation `[xi_i, xi_{i+1}] = 1`.
   145	
   146	**Verdict on the flagged inference — CONFIRMED, and sharpened.** Let `tau = (1 2)`,
   147	`tau' = (3 4)` be disjoint. Then `tau tau'` has order `2`, `(tau tau')^k in {1, tau tau'}`,
   148	and both commute with `tau`; so `(N_k)` holds for every `k >= 1`. Explicitly:
   149	`k = 1`: `[tau, tau'] = 1` (disjoint). `k = 2`: `(tau tau')^2 = 1`, so `(N_2)` is
   150	`[1, tau] = 1`. The acquisition's claim is therefore correct, and the strengthening that
   151	matters below is: **a disjoint-transposition pair satisfies the strictly stronger nodal
   152	relation `[xi_i, xi_{i+1}] = 1`, not merely `(N_k)`.** This is the hinge of §5.
   153	
   154	**Consequence (route 1's opening observation, made precise).** Every local braid of the
   155	factorization acts on the image tuple as follows: the `d-1` tangency braids identify two
   156	generators, and each `A_{2k-1}` braid acts *trivially* on the image tuple, because the
   157	Hurwitz move of `sigma_i^{2k}` is conjugation of the pair by `(tau_i tau_{i+1})^k`, an
   158	element that centralizes both. So all singular-point data are invisible to `phi`, and
   159	**any obstruction must come from the tangencies and from infinity**. That is the correct
   160	form of the acquisition's "the obstruction MUST be global".
