   164	## 3. Route 1 — the braid at infinity (Theorem A, literature-free)
   165	
   166	**3.1 The Hurwitz reformulation.** Put `T = (tau_1,...,tau_d)`, `tau_j = phi(xi_j)`. The
   167	ZvK relations say exactly that `T` is a **fixed point of the Hurwitz action** of every
   168	`rho(g_i)` on `S_4^d`, where `sigma_i` acts by
   169	`(...,t_i,t_{i+1},...) -> (..., t_i t_{i+1} t_i^{-1}, t_i, ...)`. Two standing facts:
   170	the Hurwitz action preserves the ordered product `Pi := tau_1 tau_2 ... tau_d`, and it
   171	preserves the generated subgroup `< tau_1,...,tau_d > = S_4` (surjectivity of `phi`, since
   172	the `xi_j` generate).
   173	
   174	Since `rho_infty := rho(g_N ... g_1)` lies in the braid monodromy group, `T` is fixed by
   175	`rho_infty` as well. `rho_infty` is the braid traced by the `d` roots `y_j(x)` over a
   176	large circle `|x| = R`: the **braid at infinity**.
   177	
   178	**3.2 Identification of `rho_infty` on the coprime stratum.** From §1,
   179	`y ~ const . x^{n/d}` as `x -> infty` (because `y ~ b_n t^n`, `x ~ a_d t^d`). One place at
   180	infinity with `x`-degree `d` there means the `d` roots form a single Puiseux cycle of
   181	order `d`, so:
   182	
   183	* `p(rho_infty) in S_d` (underlying permutation) is a **`d`-cycle** — unconditionally;
   184	* if `gcd(n,d) = 1`, the exponent `n/d` is already in lowest terms, the `d` conjugates
   185	  separate at first order, no further characteristic exponent is needed, and the link at
   186	  infinity is the `(d,n)` **torus knot**; up to conjugacy in `B_d`,
   187	
   188	  ```text
   189	  rho_infty = delta^n ,       delta := sigma_1 sigma_2 ... sigma_{d-1} .
   190	  ```
   191	
   192	*Consistency check (independent of the identification).* Exponent sums: the factorization
   193	of `rho_infty` has `V = d-1` tangency factors (`(V)` of §1) of exponent `1` and, at each
   194	double point, one factor of exponent `2k_p`, so `e(rho_infty) = (d-1) + 2 delta_aff`.
   195	With `(G)` and `delta_infty = (a-1)(b-1)/2 = (d-n-1)(d-1)/2` (the `(a,b)`-cusp value,
   196	`gcd(a,b) = gcd(n,d) = 1`) this is `(d-1) + (n-1)(d-1) = n(d-1) = e(delta^n)`. The two
   197	computations agree, which is a genuine cross-check on both `(V)` and the identification.
   198	It also records, for later use,
   199	
   200	```text
   201	(D)      2 delta_aff = (n-1)(d-1)          [coprime stratum] .
   202	```
   203	
   204	**3.3 The action of `delta`.** Direct computation (verified at `d = 2, 3` and then by
   205	induction on the word `sigma_1 ... sigma_{d-1}` applied right-to-left):
   206	
   207	```text
   208	delta . (t_1,...,t_d) = ( Pi t_d Pi^{-1}, t_1, ..., t_{d-1} ),     Pi = t_1 t_2 ... t_d ,
   209	```
   210	
   211	so `delta^d = Delta_d^2` acts as global conjugation by `Pi` (the classical statement that
   212	the full twist conjugates by the total boundary word).
   213	
   214	**THEOREM A.** *Let `D` be as in §1 with `gcd(n,d) = 1`, and suppose `phi` exists. Then,
   215	with `Pi = tau_1 ... tau_d in S_4`:*
   216	
   217	1. `Pi^n = 1`;
   218	2. `sgn(Pi) = (-1)^d`, hence `dn` is even: **`d` and `n` cannot both be odd**;
   219	3. `n >= 3` (so `d >= 4`), and moreover **either (`ord(Pi) = 3`, `3 | n`, `d` even) or
   220	   (`ord(Pi) = 4`, `4 | n`, `d` odd)**.
   221	
   222	*Proof.* Conjugating the whole picture we may take `rho_infty = delta^n`; the Hurwitz
   223	action preserves the ordered product, so `Pi` is unchanged by that conjugation.
   224	
   225	(1) `T` is `delta^n`-fixed, hence `delta^{nd}`-fixed. But `delta^{nd} = (delta^d)^n` acts
   226	as conjugation by `Pi^n`. So `Pi^n` centralizes every `tau_j`, hence centralizes
   227	`< tau_j > = S_4`, hence `Pi^n in Z(S_4) = 1`.
   228	
   229	(2) `Pi` is a product of `d` transpositions.  With (1), `sgn(Pi)^n = 1`, i.e.
   230	`(-1)^{dn} = 1`.
   231	
   232	(3) Extend `T` to a bi-infinite sequence by `t_j = tau_j` for `1 <= j <= d` and
   233	`t_{j-d} := Pi t_j Pi^{-1}`. By 3.3, `(delta^n T)_j = t_{j-n}`, so `delta^n`-fixedness is
   234	`t_j = t_{j-n}` for all `j`: the sequence is `n`-periodic. Let `r := d mod n`; then
   235	`gcd(r,n) = gcd(d,n) = 1` and `n`-periodicity turns `t_{j-d} = Pi t_j Pi^{-1}` into
   236	`t_{j-r} = Pi t_j Pi^{-1}`. So conjugation by `Pi` acts on the `n`-periodic sequence as
   237	the shift by `-r`, which generates `Z/n`. Hence `{tau_1,...,tau_n}` is a **single
   238	conjugation orbit of `tau_1` under `<Pi>`**; and since `d > n` and the sequence is
   239	`n`-periodic, `{tau_1,...,tau_n} = {tau_1,...,tau_d}`, which generates `S_4`.
   240	Now enumerate `g in S_4` by whether the `<g>`-conjugation orbit of some transposition
   241	generates `S_4`: `g = 1`, `g` a transposition, `g` a double transposition all give orbits
   242	of size `<= 2`, generating a subgroup of order `<= 6` — impossible. `g` a `3`-cycle:
   243	orbit `{(14),(24),(34)}` works (orbit `{(12),(23),(13)}` gives `S_3` only). `g` a
   244	`4`-cycle: orbit `{(12),(23),(34),(14)}` works. So `ord(Pi) in {3,4}`; with `Pi^n = 1`
   245	this gives `3 | n` resp. `4 | n`, and `sgn(Pi) = +1` resp. `-1` combined with (2) gives
   246	`d` even resp. `d` odd. Finally `S_4` needs at least `3` transpositions to generate, so
   247	`n >= 3`; and `d > n` gives `d >= 4`. `[]`
